What does not: a Notion integration token and API keys (for example a Fathom or Gojiberry key without a connector), the same as in Claude Code with this edition.
@@ replace
Also read the current state. When step 0 chose the project folder, put `cd <project folder> &&` in front of each command, so the project's `.gtm` folder is found:

```
python3 -I -B ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/lib/config.py paths
python3 -I -B ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/lib/config.py show
```

On a first run there is no config yet, so `paths` prints the default home folder (`source` `home`, `dir` `~/.config/gtm`) and `show` prints template defaults. That is expected: ignore that folder when step 0 chose the project folder, and write nothing there. Step 5 creates the config in the chosen folder.
@@ with
Also read the current state. Run the folder command (`${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/reference/config.md`, No Python, step 1); when step 0 chose the project folder, put `cd <project folder> &&` in front of it, so the project's `.gtm` folder is found. Then Read `config.json` in that folder, when it exists.

On a first run there is no config yet, so the command prints the default home folder (`~/.config/gtm`). That is expected: ignore that folder when step 0 chose the project folder, and write nothing there. Step 5 creates the config in the chosen folder from `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/templates/config.example.json`.

A config written by Tessary GTM Pro works as it is. Keep its values, also `notion.access` = `rest`, and never touch its `credentials.json`.
